window.DEVLOG_INDEX = [{"t": "First footage, and the take that filmed the wrong window", "s": "The Engine session proved the whole pipeline in 11 minutes: kits, live C++ compiles, DLSS and filming the game by itself. Its first clip recorded the wrong window.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0640-first-footage.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Engine"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794465360.0, "i": "media/first-footage-template-map.thumb.jpg", "x": "This isn't the game yet. It's Unreal's stock first-person template, driven by injected input, filmed by the Engine session with nobody at the keyboard. Nothing in this clip will ship. What matters is that the crew can now run something, move in it and film it on its own, which is how every later task gets checked. Three cuts from the Engine session's test clip: walk to the cylinder, turn, walk the wall. The input is scripted. Take one The first clip didn't show the game at all. The recorder had grabbed a different window on my desktop. The Engine session spotted it, deleted the clip and its contact sheets, and sent the Devlog and the Director an urgent message before anything was copied: \"If you copied any of them (devlog repo, drafts, site), delete them and don't publish.\" Nothing had been. The capture setup now records the game window directly, the clip was re-shot three minutes later, and the Toolsmith was asked to fix the recording script so it can't happen again. Before this clip went on the site, a reviewer checked all 872 frames for anything that wasn't the game. That's the \"don't suffer silently\" rule doing its job in hour 2: say what went wrong at once, fix it, write it down. What the baseline check proved The Engine session's first task: six checks, two with caveats. Task T-0001 took 11 minutes, from 06:08 to 06:19. The summary in the Engine's own words: \"Baseline PASS with caveats.\" Kits work. A throwaway kit created one material in the running editor and was delet"}, {"t": "Pitch locked: Reel Time, nearly three hours early", "s": "The first milestone landed at 06:08, 68 minutes into the jam. Minutes later the Director had five contracts and twelve tasks written and five sessions building.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0615-pitch-locked.html", "c": ["Milestones", "Behind the scenes"], "w": ["Me", "Director"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794464100.0, "i": "media/pitch-locked-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "The first milestone, \"Pitch locked\", was due at 09:00. It was done at 06:08, in hour 2. It went that fast because the thinking was already done. Wednesday's Q&A had become a kickoff brief (see Pre-production ). This morning the Director read it, drafted the design overview, and read back four points for me to confirm. My whole answer: \"1, if you think ours is gonna be better with more control go for it. 2 correct. 3 yes, 4 i would like to playtest that this evening. [...] but for now, go\" What that decided The pitch is locked as written, under the working title Reel Time. The replay is our own system. Unreal's built-in replay records network replication, reloads the level to play back, and doesn't replay ragdolls or heavy time scaling faithfully. Ours records each take as samples and events, then replays it with puppets and an automatic edit. DLSS ships as a settings dropdown, and we go for the NVIDIA RTX Award as a bonus. The port was already done. I playtest the greybox loop this evening. Contracts before code Before any code existed, the Director wrote five short contracts: events, the reel, levels, NPCs and UI data. Each says who provides a system and what everyone else can build against, so gameplay, world and the rest can start in parallel instead of waiting for each other. The Director logged this one as an assumption, not a decision: the gameplay session may change names as it builds, as long as it reports them. The rule at the centre of the events contract: Every cha"}, {"t": "Porting NVIDIA DLSS from Unreal 5.8 to 5.9 in 75 minutes", "s": "NVIDIA's DLSS 4.5 plugin stops at Unreal 5.8. Six compiles, three editor launches and one crash later it runs on our 5.9 engine, without Frame Generation.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0605-dlss-on-unreal-5-9.html", "c": ["Tools and pipeline", "Bloopers"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 2", "o": 1794463500.0, "i": "media/dlss-six-compiles.thumb.jpg", "x": "I asked for this one to be written up \"even if it doesn't work\". It mostly worked. Why bother. The NVIDIA RTX Award needs an entry that uses DLSS 4.5, an NvRTX feature or NVIDIA ACE. DLSS is the only realistic one for an RTX 2060. NVIDIA's plugin (v8.9.0) supports Unreal 5.5 to 5.8, and the jam engine is a 5.9 source build. The plugin ships with full source, so a port was possible. Claude and I did it on Wednesday 11 November, before the jam, as tooling. It's declared on the Made before the jam page. Six compiles The download is 1.1 GB with 11 plugins. We took five of them (20 C++ modules) into an empty test project, so the real project was never at risk. Six compile attempts. The first two failures were ours, the next three were one Unreal 5.9 change, and the sixth passed. Attempt 1: a quoting mistake in the build command. Ours, not NVIDIA's. Attempt 2: Windows' 260-character path limit. The test folder's path was long enough that build files went past it. Still a thing in 2026. Attempt 3: two minutes in, most of it compiled. Every failure was the same thing: Unreal 5.9 checks log-message format strings at compile time, and the 5.8 code passes 64-bit numbers and enum values to %d and %u . Attempts 4 and 5: more of the same, patched by hand each time. Attempt 6: \"Result: Succeeded\" in 2 minutes 16 seconds. The fix we should have looked for first Three compiles of hand patches, then the switch Unreal 5.9 provides for exactly this. After patching 12 log messages one at a time, "}, {"t": "Pre-production: everything that existed before the clock", "s": "Two evenings of setup before the jam: a crew of Claude Code sessions, a smoke test that found 11 problems, the rules, the theme reveal and a Q&A. No game.", "u": "posts/2026-11-12-0555-pre-production.html", "c": ["Behind the scenes", "Tools and pipeline"], "w": ["Me"], "d": "Day 1, hour 1", "o": 1794462900.0, "i": "media/preprod-slate.thumb.jpg", "x": "I'm Daniel Johnson, and for the next 93 hours I'm making a game called Reel Time with a crew of Claude Code sessions. This post is the one-stop record of what existed before the clock started at 05:00 on Thursday 12 November. The short version: tooling, plans and one engine plugin. No game. The jam's rules allow exactly that (§7(h)): \"Entrants may prepare AI tools, agent configurations, Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\") settings, prompt libraries, and scripts before the Contest Period. [...] All other game content, including gameplay, levels, and art assets (other than noted sourced assets), must be created during the Contest Period.\" Two evenings of setup, then the clock. The game project's first commit is at 05:27 on Thursday, 27 minutes into the jam. The crew Each Claude Code session has one job, its own folders and an inbox folder. I talk to two of them: the Director, who turns what I say into tasks, and the Devlog, who writes this site. Only the Engine session touches the Unreal Editor. Everyone else hands it a \"kit\", a script that builds their assets and can be rerun. Who talks to whom. Every arrow is a file dropped in an inbox folder. A task's journey, from a request to something a player can see. In numbers: 12 role files, 28 coordination scripts, 7 hooks, 3 subagents and 2 skills. By day the Director and Devlog run on Claude Fable 5.2 and most builders on Opus 5.5. Anything done on a smaller model has to be reviewed at Opus level before it counts as done. The smoke test"}];
